#: Game.Levels.ClassicalCiphers.L05_Substitution
msgid "## Beyond a Single Shift\n"
"\n"
"The Caesar cipher only ever shifts every letter by the *same* amount §0.\n"
"A general **substitution cipher** scrambles the alphabet by *any* rule at\n"
"all — as long as that rule never sends two different letters to the same\n"
"place, so decryption is possible.\n"
"\n"
"'Never sends two different letters to the same place, and hits every\n"
"letter' is exactly what Mathlib calls a **permutation**: §1\n"
"is the type of bijections from the alphabet to itself, bundled together\n"
"with their own inverse. A permutation doesn't need the ring structure\n"
"(addition, multiplication) that §2 carries — just 26 distinguishable\n"
"letters — so this level uses the plainer §3 instead; they're the\n"
"same 26 elements underneath, viewed with only as much structure as the\n"
"task actually needs.\n"
"\n"
"### Your Task\n"
"\n"
"Given any substitution rule §4 (not just a shift!)\n"
"and its inverse §5, prove that decrypting an encrypted message\n"
"recovers the original: §6.\n"
"\n"
"### Strategy\n"
"\n"
"This is exactly what it means for §7 to be a two-sided inverse of §8\n"
"— Mathlib has a lemma for precisely this."
msgstr ""

